# Line endings.
#
# This is a Windows working tree with core.autocrlf=true, so git checks most
# files out as CRLF. For source that is fine. For a file that is EXECUTED BY
# LINUX it is not: a shell script with CRLF fails as
#
#   /bin/sh: bad interpreter: /bin/sh^M
#
# and `scripts/backup.sh` is bind-mounted straight from the working tree into
# the backup container by docker-compose.prod.yml. It would have been broken on
# every Windows checkout while looking correct in the repository, because the
# stored blob is LF and only the checkout differs.

* text=auto

# Executed by Linux — must be LF wherever they are checked out.
*.sh           text eol=lf
gradlew        text eol=lf
Dockerfile     text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile   text eol=lf

# Executed by Windows — must be CRLF.
*.bat          text eol=crlf
*.cmd          text eol=crlf
gradlew.bat    text eol=crlf

# Never touched. Normalising these corrupts them.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.pdf    binary
*.pptx   binary
*.docx   binary
*.zip    binary
*.jar    binary
*.pt     binary
*.onnx   binary
*.keystore binary
